
This directory is intended for project header files.

A header file is a file containing C declarations and macro definitions
to be shared between several project source files. You request the use of a
header file in your project source file (C, C++, etc) located in `src` folder
by including it, with the C preprocessing directive `#include'.

```src/main.c

#include "header.h"

int main (void)
{
 ...
}
```

Including a header file produces the same results as copying the header file
into each source file that needs it. Such copying would be time-consuming
and error-prone. With a header file, the related declarations appear
in only one place. If they need to be changed, they can be changed in one
place, and programs that include the header file will automatically use the
new version when next recompiled. The header file eliminates the labor of
finding and changing all the copies as well as the risk that a failure to
find one copy will result in inconsistencies within a program.

In C, the convention is to give header files names that end with `.h'.

Read more about using header files in official GCC documentation:

* Include Syntax
* Include Operation
* Once-Only Headers
* Computed Includes

https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/cpp/Header-Files.html

---

# Sensor Interface (C++ / Arduino)

This project uses a small C++ interface (`ISensor`) to keep sensor code consistent and easy to extend. Each sensor (DHT, flame, etc.) implements the same lifecycle and sampling API, so the main loop can treat all sensors uniformly.

## Interface: `ISensor`

File: `include/ISensor.h`

Each sensor must implement:

* `const char* name() const`
  Human-readable identifier for logs and debugging.

* `bool begin()`
  Initialize hardware/library state. Return `true` if initialization succeeds.

* `bool ready() const`
  Indicates whether the sensor is currently able to provide data (useful for warmup-time sensors).

* `bool sample()`
  Capture/update a reading. Return `true` if a new reading was produced.
  Sensors should self-throttle internally if they require minimum read intervals (example: DHT).

* `uint32_t ageMs() const`
  Age of the last valid reading in milliseconds. Use `UINT32_MAX` if no reading has been captured yet.

* `bool healthy() const`
  `true` if the sensor is functioning normally; `false` after repeated read failures or detected faults.

## Folder placement

* `include/`
  Shared headers and interfaces (`ISensor.h`, packet structs, pin maps)

* `src/`
  Application logic (state machine, scheduling, LoRa comms)

* `lib/` (optional)
  Reusable modules if we later want to share code across multiple firmware projects (drone node, base station)

## Usage pattern

Sensors are registered as `ISensor*` and sampled in a loop:

* Call `begin()` once in `setup()`
* Call `sample()` frequently in `loop()`
* Print/log/transmit values at a slower cadence

This keeps the main program simple while allowing each sensor implementation to manage its own timing and failure handling.
